menu "Open Lounge Phone"

config OLP_SERVER_URL
    string "Server (WebSocket base URL; empty = none, chosen on the setup page)"
    default ""
    help
        Base URL of the Open Lounge Phone server; the phone connects to <url>/ws/device.
        Empty (the default): the phone connects to nothing until someone chooses a server on the
        setup page (your own, or the public hub to try it), with MENU -> 4 or the console
        `server` command (both stored in NVS). Set it to bake your own server into your builds.

config OLP_WIFI_SSID
    string "Default Wi-Fi SSID"
    default ""
    help
        Used when no Wi-Fi is stored in NVS (console: `wifi <ssid> <pass>`).

config OLP_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Default Wi-Fi password"
    default ""

config OLP_SIM
    bool "Build for a simulator (Wokwi or QEMU)"
    default n
    help
        Moves keys 6 and 7 off the UART0 pins (the simulator's console) and uses the test audio
        device (no ES8311 in a simulator).

config OLP_QEMU
    bool "Build for QEMU (Espressif's esp32s3 machine)"
    depends on OLP_SIM
    default n
    select ETH_USE_OPENETH
    help
        QEMU has no Wi-Fi: the network is its emulated OpenCores Ethernet (user-mode NAT, see
        tools/qemu.sh). There is no display either: the framebuffer is kept for `screen`.

config OLP_STORAGE_ENCRYPTED
    bool "Encrypt NVS (device key, Wi-Fi) with the eFuse HMAC key: release builds"
    default n
    select NVS_ENCRYPTION
    help
        ESP-IDF's HMAC-based NVS encryption (no flash encryption needed). ON in release builds
        (sdkconfig.release, which also sets NVS_SEC_HMAC_EFUSE_KEY_ID=5), OFF for development and
        the simulators. The first boot with it BURNS a random key into eFuse KEY5: one-time and
        irreversible (README "Encrypted storage").

menu "Updates (OTA)"

config OLP_BOARD_ID
    string "Board id (updates must be signed for it)"
    default "minimal-revA"
    help
        The board this firmware runs on (hardware release hw-v0.1 = the minimal board, rev A).
        A signed manifest for another board is refused.

config OLP_OTA
    bool "Firmware updates over the air (needs your own update key)"
    default n
    help
        Off by default: a phone built from source never checks for updates. Turn it on to update
        your phones from your own channel: `tools/release.sh keygen` makes your key pair in
        keys/ (gitignored), and the manifest URL below (or `ota url`) says where to look.
        Official release builds (sdkconfig.release) turn it on with the project's release key
        and channel. README "Your keys, your server".

config OLP_OTA_MANIFEST_URL
    string "Firmware update channel (manifest URL; empty = set later with `ota url`)"
    depends on OLP_OTA
    default ""
    help
        A fixed URL whose signed manifest names the newest firmware, e.g. your fork's
        https://github.com/<you>/<repo>/releases/download/fw-stable/firmware-manifest.json
        (tools/release.sh publish --repo <you>/<repo> keeps it current). Never /releases/latest:
        that is whatever release is newest, a hardware release (hw-v*) included. The console
        `ota url <url>` overrides it (NVS).

config OLP_OTA_PUBKEY
    string "Update public key (PEM, relative to firmware/)"
    depends on OLP_OTA
    default "keys/ota_signing_pub.pem"
    help
        The key whose signature a manifest must carry (RSA-3072, RSA-PSS/SHA-256; the same key
        signs release images). `tools/release.sh keygen` writes yours here. Official release
        builds use the project's key, main/ota_signing_pub.pem. The build stops if the file is
        missing.

config OLP_OTA_NIGHT_ONLY
    bool "Install automatic updates only at night (02:00-05:00 local)"
    depends on OLP_OTA
    default y
    help
        Updates install only while the phone is hung up and idle (10 min) either way. Local time
        comes from SNTP and the space's UTC offset in `config`. MENU -> 9 installs at once.

config OLP_TEST_CRASH_AT_BOOT
    bool "TEST ONLY: crash at boot (the OTA rollback test's broken image)"
    default n

endmenu

menu "Call audio"

config OLP_EARPIECE_MAX_DB
    int "Earpiece volume cap (dBFS)"
    range -30 0
    default -6
    help
        The loudest the earpiece plays (volume 10/10). Full scale reaches about 98 dB SPL with
        the assumed receiver (hardware/SCHEMATIC_REVIEW.md, owner question 8), so the default
        cap is -6 dBFS until the handset's receiver is measured. Each volume step is 3 dB.

config OLP_MIC_PGA_DB
    int "Handset mic PGA gain (dB, 0-30 in 3 dB steps)"
    range 0 30
    default 18
    help
        ES8311 PGA gain for the handset electret (hardware sim b07 assumed 18 dB).

config OLP_AUDIO_TEST_DEVICE
    bool "Use the test audio device (1 kHz source, analysing sink) instead of the ES8311"
    default y if OLP_SIM
    default n
    help
        The simulator has no ES8311. On a board without a codec the firmware falls back to the
        test device anyway.

config OLP_ICE_TCP_RELAY
    bool "TURN over TCP/TLS as well as UDP (experimental: broken in esp_peer 1.5.6)"
    default n
    help
        esp_peer uses one TURN server over UDP and one over TCP or TLS. In esp_peer 1.5.6 the
        TCP/TLS relay doesn't work (TLS gives up before the TCP connect finishes; over TCP, ICE
        pairs but DTLS reads fail) and listing it stalls candidate gathering, so calls use STUN +
        TURN over UDP only. Turn this on to try a newer esp_peer. `ice tcp` / `ice tls` on the
        console try it for the next call.

config OLP_ICE_PREFER_TLS
    bool "With the TCP/TLS relay: prefer TURN over TLS on port 443"
    depends on OLP_ICE_TCP_RELAY
    default y

config OLP_RTC_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "ICE agent receive timeout (ms)"
    default 300 if OLP_SIM
    default 100

config OLP_RTC_TASK_STACK
    int "WebRTC task stack (bytes)"
    default 16384

endmenu

endmenu
